Q: NullPointerException is logged different in prod and local env A: <p>Most likely you have compiled the jars with different debug level for use in local vs production. If you want same behaviour use the same jars (assuming that you don't put prod/dev settings in each one!)</p>
<p>Check the value of javac flag <code>-Djavac.debuglevel</code>.
This demonstrates the difference:</p>
<pre><code>public class Main {
    public static void main(String... args) {
        call(null);
    }
    private static void call(String [] c) {
        System.out.println(&quot;c.length &quot;+c.length);
    }
}

</code></pre>
<p>On dev servers I normally use <code>javac -Djavac.debug=true -Djavac.debuglevel=lines,vars,source</code> to provide more accurate stack traces with line and variable details, and this program would print:</p>
<pre><code>Exception in thread &quot;main&quot; java.lang.NullPointerException: 
  Cannot read the array length because &quot;c&quot; is null
    at stackoverflow.Main.call(Main.java:12)
    at stackoverflow.Main.main(Main.java:9)
</code></pre>
<p>When compiled with <code>javac -Djavac.debug=false</code> this code prints:</p>
<pre><code>Exception in thread &quot;main&quot; java.lang.NullPointerException: 
  Cannot read the array length because &quot;&lt;parameter1&gt;&quot; is null
    at stackoverflow.Main.call(Unknown Source)
    at stackoverflow.Main.main(Unknown Source)
</code></pre>

Q: How to convert an string to unsigned string in c A: <p>What won't work:</p>
<p>Casting to <code>(unsigned)</code> without a type specified means <code>unsigned int</code>, so you are casting the address of the array to an <code>unsigned int</code>, which is plain wrong.</p>
<p>Remember that when we use an array name in an expression, it &quot;decays&quot; to a pointer to the first element in that array. So <code>string_hexadecimal</code> gives us an address, with a pointer type.</p>
<p>So even if you used <code>(unsigned char)</code> that wouldn't work either, because you don't want to cast the <em>address</em> to another type no matter.</p>
<p>And casting to <code>(unsigned char*)</code> doesn't work either, but this time because you can't assign to arrays in C - the language is simply designed in that way. Nor can you initialize an array with a pointer.</p>
<hr />
<p>Solution:</p>
<p>In C it is safe to &quot;type pun&quot; between any of the character types, so simply use a union. Then you don't allocate the array twice either, saving memory. This is the correct approach when you want to represent the same memory area using various different types.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

typedef union
{
  char          c_hex [100];
  unsigned char u_hex [100];
} hexstr_t;


int main()
{
  hexstr_t hexstr =
  {
    .c_hex = &quot;5a2be26e5ef51ad833c153&quot;
  };

  // then do something with hexstr.u_hex
}
</code></pre>

Q: &quot;message&quot;:&quot;Error: TypeError: (intermediate value) is not iterable&quot; A: <p>The connection apparently returns <code>undefined</code> or <code>null</code> value. Those values are not <em>iterable</em>, you can't use <em>destructuring</em> on <code>undefined</code>. You could add a <em>fallback</em> value in case if the response is <code>undefined</code> or <code>null</code>.</p>
<pre><code>const response = await connection.execute('USE Music;SELECT * FROM artist;');

const [rows, fields] = response || [];
                            // ^^^^^^^ secure possibility that response is undefined

return {
    statusCode: 200,
    body: JSON.stringify(rows)
};
</code></pre>
